-macOS keeps Accessibility, Event Posting, input listening, and Screen Recording in separate TCC buckets. One successful grant does not prove the others are usable. OpenClaw's Computer Control status checks Accessibility, Event Posting, and Screen Recording separately; this is why screenshots can succeed while clicks and typing fail.
+macOS keeps Accessibility, Event Posting, input listening, and Screen Recording in separate TCC buckets. One successful grant does not prove the others are usable. Screenshots can succeed while clicks and typing fail.
 
-An Accessibility row can also remain visibly enabled while its code requirement is pinned to an older build. When OpenClaw reports **Accessibility grant may be stale**, select OpenClaw under **System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Accessibility**, remove it with **-**, then re-add `/Applications/OpenClaw.app`. Quit and reopen OpenClaw afterward because Accessibility trust can remain cached in the running process.
+For screen-coordinate Computer Control actions using the embedded Peekaboo provider, the running native app checks Accessibility and Event Posting before resolving the display or posting input. Its permission snapshot also checks Screen Recording. If input access is denied, the error reports every missing bucket in that snapshot. `ACCESSIBILITY_REQUIRED` takes precedence when Accessibility is missing, including when Event Posting is also missing; `POST_EVENT_REQUIRED` means Accessibility passed but Event Posting did not. Screen Recording alone does not determine input eligibility.
+
+**Dashboard → Settings → This Mac → Permissions** and the node's advertised permission map do not expose Event Posting. The bundled `openclaw-mac status` command does not report these native permission booleans either. There is no separate three-bucket Computer Control status view; an Accessibility checkmark or an earlier node status is not proof that the current Event Posting check will pass.
+
+Screen Recording success with Accessibility denial does **not** establish an old build, a stale code requirement, or the cause of the denial. Verify the running signed app and its actual permission results before attributing the problem to identity changes. Check that exact app under **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Accessibility**, complete any macOS authentication, and verify access again after changing the grant. If macOS requires reopening the app, reopen the same signed app and recheck. An unchanged denial is not evidence that another re-add or restart will help.
 
